<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thepphiphit</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Prince Thepphiphit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thepphiphit was a Siamese prince, a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommakot">Borommakot</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, who was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> after involvement in court intrigue. He later returned to Siam, and after the fall of Ayutthaya in 1767 he set himself up as ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau">Khorat Plateau</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> defeated his forces in 1768, and he was captured and executed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>A Chinese source puts his age at about fifty when he died, so he was probably born around 1718. Known at birth as Prince Khaek, he was a son of Prince Phon by a secondary consort whom the same source calls Baitou, suggesting Northern Thai or Lao origin. Prince Phon won the succession war of 1733 and became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommakot">Borommakot</a>, who gave his sons manpower regiments and ranks by their mothers' standing: Khaek became Kromma Muen Thepphiphit, a lower rank than his half-brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekkathat">Ekkathat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthumphon">Uthumphon</a>. In 1757 Thepphiphit and senior ministers persuaded Borommakot to make Uthumphon heir in place of Ekkathat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekkathat">Ekkathat</a> nonetheless took the throne. In December 1758 ministers plotting to replace him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthumphon">Uthumphon</a> approached Thepphiphit, then a monk at Wat Krachom. Uthumphon betrayed the plan to Ekkathat in return for the plotters' lives, and Thepphiphit, deserted by his followers, fled west and was caught in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchanaburi">Kanchanaburi</a> province. Spared at Uthumphon's request, he was sent with his family on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in early 1759. There Sinhalese monks and nobles plotted in 1760 to put him on the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> in place of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirti-sri-rajasinha">Kirti Sri Rajasinha</a>; the plan failed and he had to leave.</p><p>He landed at Mergui in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> in 1762, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekkathat">Ekkathat</a> kept him confined. When the Burmese took Mergui in January 1765 he was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanthaburi">Chanthaburi</a>. In mid-1766, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> under Burmese siege, he gathered 2,000 men of eastern Siam at Paknam Yothaka near Prachinburi, but his force was defeated. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima">Nakhon Ratchasima</a>, where his son Prince Prayong killed the hostile governor on 18 September 1766 and seized the city, and he was later held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a>. After Ayutthaya fell in April 1767, local officials proclaimed him Chao Phimai, Lord of Phimai, one of several regional regimes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1768 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> of Thonburi marched against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a>, and Thepphiphit and his family were captured and taken to Thonburi. According to the Thai chronicles he refused to bow before Taksin, who reportedly told him that wherever he went his supporters died, and he was executed late in 1768. His daughter, Princess Ubon, became one of Taksin's consorts and was executed on 20 June 1769 after being accused of adultery; his two surviving young sons, Prince Mongkhon and Prince Lamduan, were spared.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kromma Muen Thepphiphit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Phimai, on the Khorat plateau, <b>Prince Thepphiphit</b>, a son of King Borommakot who had earlier been exiled to Ceylon, gathered followers and claimed authority as the legitimate heir.</blockquote><a href="/state/phimai-regime">Phimai regime</a></li></ul></section>
